        136.71470642089844,
        522.7216796875,
        234.95361328125
      ],
      "text": "When we experience stressful events, a physiological change is triggered that\ncauses us to take in warnings and focus on what might go wrong. Brain imaging\nreveals that this 'switch' is related to a sudden boost in a neural signal\nimportant for learning, specifically in response to unexpected warning signs,\nsuch as faces expressing fear.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        279.87469482421875,
        505.768798828125,
        457.6736145019531
      ],
      "text": "Such neural engineering could have helped prehistoric humans to survive.\nWhen our ancestors found themselves surrounded by hungry animals, they\nwould have benefited from an increased ability to learn about hazards. In a\nsafe environment, however, it would have been wasteful to be on high alert\nconstantly. So, a neural switch that automatically increases or decreases our\nability to process warnings in response to changes in our environment could\nhave been useful. In fact, people with clinical depression and anxiety seem\nunable to switch away from a state in which they absorb all the negative\nmessages around them.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        502.5936584472656,
        518.5401611328125,
        660.4736328125
      ],
      "text": "It is also important to realise that stress travels rapidly from one person to the\nnext. If a co-worker is stressed, we are more likely to tense up and feel\nstressed ourselves. We don't even need to be in the same room with someone\nfor their emotions to influence our behaviour. Studies show that if we observe\npositive feeds on social media, such as images of a pink sunset, we are more\nlikely to post uplifting messages ourselves. If we observe negative posts, such\nas complaints about a long queue at the coffee shop, we will in turn create\nmore negative posts.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
